# Ingestion Enhancements: Deep Email Forensics & Attachment Detonation

## Goal Description
Upgrade the Ingestion Engine to perform deep forensic analysis on incoming artifacts.
1.  **Deep Email Forensics**: Implement independent cryptographic verification of DKIM and DMARC signatures for raw emails, rather than relying solely on upstream headers.
2.  **Attachment Detonation (Stage 1)**: Implement recursive extraction for composite file types (Zip, Nested Emails) to expose hidden payloads.

## User Review Required
> [!IMPORTANT]
> This plan introduces extensive use of the `github.com/emersion/go-msgauth` library for standard-compliant verification.
> This ensures we don't roll our own crypto/parsing logic for sensitive checks.

> [!WARNING]
> Recursive attachment processing (Zip bombs) will be capped at a depth of 3 and a max file limit of 10 to prevent DoS.

## Proposed Changes

### Module: Ingestion Engine

#### [NEW] [parser/verifier.go](file:///c:/Users/Kandukoori Tejaswini/Desktop/PhishVault2/PhishVault/services/ingestion/parser/verifier.go)
- **Purpose**: dedicated file for cryptographic verifications.
- `VerifyDKIM(r io.Reader) (AuthResult, error)`: Verifies DKIM signatures.
- `VerifySPF(ip string, domain string) (AuthResult, error)`: Performs SPF DNS lookup (if sender IP is available).
- `VerifyDMARC(domain string, spfResult, dkimResult) (AuthResult, error)`: Checks DMARC records.
- Note: We will focus on DKIM first as it acts on the *content*. SPF requires the *sender IP* which is often lost in "upload raw file" scenarios (unless we simulate/trust headers).

#### [MODIFY] [engine/email_processor.go](file:///c:/Users/Kandukoori Tejaswini/Desktop/PhishVault2/PhishVault/services/ingestion/engine/email_processor.go)
- Integrate `parser.VerifyDKIM` logic.
- Store `auth_dkim_independent` in metadata.
- If the email contains nested generic attachments that are actually other emails (`message/rfc822`), flag them for recursive processing.

#### [MODIFY] [engine/attachment_processor.go](file:///c:/Users/Kandukoori Tejaswini/Desktop/PhishVault2/PhishVault/services/ingestion/engine/attachment_processor.go)
- **Recursive Processing**:
    - Detect `application/zip` and `application/x-tar`.
    - Use `archive/zip` to read standard archives.
    - Extract files (in-memory if small) and feed them back into `ArtifactFactory.Ingest()` as children.
    - **Guards**: Max Depth = 3. Max Files = 10. Max Size = 10MB per child.
- **Office Docs**:
    - Detect `application/msword` or `.docx`.
    - Flag "Macros Potential" (simple byte sequence check for now).

#### [MODIFY] [engine/factory.go](file:///c:/Users/Kandukoori Tejaswini/Desktop/PhishVault2/PhishVault/services/ingestion/engine/factory.go)
- Allow `Ingest` to accept a `depth` parameter to track recursion.

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
- **Unit Tests**:
    - `verifier_test.go`: Test with a sample signed `.eml` (we can generate one or use a fixture).
    - `attachment_recursion_test.go`: Create a zip file containing a text file. Submit it and verify 2 artifacts are created (Parent Zip + Child Text).
    - `email_recursion_test.go`: Submit an email with an attached email. Verify 2 Email artifacts.

### Manual Verification
1.  **DKIM Check**:
    - Download a real legitimate email (e.g. from Gmail) as `.eml`.
    - Submit to `/submit-email`.
    - Check DB/Logs: verify `auth_dkim_independent` is "PASS".
    - Tamper with the `.eml` body (change one character).
    - Submit again.
    - Check DB/Logs: verify `auth_dkim_independent` is "FAIL".
2.  **Zip Detonation**:
    - Create `payload.txt` ("evil code").
    - Zip it into `malware.zip`.
    - Attach `malware.zip` to an email.
    - Submit email.
    - Verify PhishVault extracts: [Email](file:///c:/Users/Kandukoori%20Tejaswini/Desktop/PhishVault2/PhishVault/services/ingestion/engine/email_processor.go#17-18) -> `malware.zip` -> `payload.txt`.
